.hgk\rounds\R5R-20261010-s4-ce-repair-and-codex-verify\verify-codex-2\raw_transcript.txt:5488:      "evidence": "Read validate.py, pipeline.py and registry.py directly from the new wheel; compared with published bytes and pre-repair tracked source, without executing the superseded wheel. NON_RECORD_SIDECARS is now exactly (_profile_meta,), while HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS retains the old six-field hash exclusion set and is shared by pipeline._seal and validator._body_hash. validate_record preserves every other field for schema validation and retains its declared-property guard. All 19 packaged schemas are byte-identical to published; all declare all four identity fields. For those schemas, shrinking the stripping set changes only the acceptance of undeclared trace, and rejects no schema-legitimate input previously accepted. PI-PKG trace remains declared and its original compiler trace passes. _profile_meta is genuinely operator/profile metadata added after record sealing, absent from all 19 record schemas, and excluded from the digest; tolerance is NOT a PI-PKG special case. Adding it to PD-PKG, ECP or TQAEP also passes with unchanged hashes. A workspace schema declaring _profile_meta as string correctly rejects the compiler object sidecar instead of stripping it. Observation disclosed: PI-PKG trace has schema {}, so an object-shaped trace also passes both new and published validators (published probe removes only the known sidecar to isolate behavior). This unchanged declared-property permissiveness is not the undeclared-trace widening."
