werShell reconnaissance: unset PYTHONPATH/PYTHONHOME; inspected branch/status, expected wheel hash, previous transcript and scratch harness, registry/validator/pipeline sources; no AGENTS.md found in workspace or ancestors. Authoritative command evidence follows.\r\nSUBJECT SHA256 d6c3e295f817b50108b1232a120564c18b517864ad1ad19c4f8d915f396d364f\r\nCOMMAND: unset PYTHONPATH; unset PYTHONHOME; git branch --show-current\r\nCWD: C:/Projects/Agent_Workspace/PIPD-r5r-repair-wt\r\nSTDOUT:\r\nr5r-ce-repair\r\n\r\nSTDERR:\r\n\r\nEXIT CODE: 0\r\n\r\nCOMMAND: unset PYTHONPATH; unset PYTHONHOME; git rev-parse HEAD\r\nCWD: C:/Projects/Agent_Workspace/PIPD-r5r-repair-wt\r\nSTDOUT:\r\n6f9d87f7bbae8262794b1c26ce735125fc656c31\r\n\r\nSTDERR:\r\n\r\nEXIT CODE: 0\r\n\r\nCOMMAND: unset PYTHONPATH; unset PYTHONHOME; git status --short\r\nCWD: C:/Projects/Agent_Workspace/PIPD-r5r-repair-wt\r\nSTDOUT:\r\n M .hgk/artifacts/s1/TECHNOLOGY_ADMISSIONS.json\r\n?? .hgk/artifacts/tqaep_design_tmp/\r\n?? .hgk/rounds/R5R-20261010-s4-ce-repair-and-codex-verify/verify-codex-2/\r\n?? .hgk/rounds/R5R-20261010-s4-ce-repair-and-codex-verify/workorder/WO-R5R-CODEXVERIFY-005-FINAL.md\r\n\r\nSTDERR:\r\n\r\nEXIT CODE: 0\r\n\r\nCOMMAND: unset PYTHONPATH; unset PYTHONHOME; git diff 6f40174da146067620a76e018f11a28b5ad54356 -- src/pipd_ls_sp/validate.py src/pipd_ls_sp/pipeline.py\r\nCWD: C:/Projects/Agent_Workspace/PIPD-r5r-repair-wt\r\nSTDOUT:\r\ndiff --git a/src/pipd_ls_sp/pipeline.py b/src/pipd_ls_sp/pipeline.py\r\nindex 01aeaa2..9746b3c 100644\r\n--- a/src/pipd_ls_sp/pipeline.py\r\n+++ b/src/pipd_ls_sp/pipeline.py\r\n@@ -16,10 +16,11 @@ from .profiles import compute_profile\r\n from . import repo_context as repo_probe\r\n from . import requirements as req_compile\r\n from .util import canonical_json, content_id, sha256_text  # noqa: F401\r\n-# CE-2: the non-record sidecar list is defined ONCE (validate.NON_RECORD_SIDECARS) and\r\n-# referenced here, so the \"stripped before validation\" contract cannot diverge from the\r\n-# validator's schema check again.\r\n-from .validate import NON_RECORD_SIDECARS\r\n+# CE-2: the field lists are defined ONCE in validate and referenced here, so the hash body and\r\n+# the \"stripped before validation\" contract cannot diverge from the validator again.\r\n+#   HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS - the fields `_seal` does not hash (identity + derived `trace` + sidecar).\r\n+#   NON_RECORD_SIDECARS  - re-exported (validate defines it); the fields the schema check strips.\r\n+from .validate import HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS, NON_RECORD_SIDECARS  # noqa: F401\r\n \r\n CLAIM_LADDER = [\"PROMPT_COMPILE_PASS\", \"HGK_ADMITTED\", \"RUNTIME_READY\", \"LOCAL_QUALIFIED\",\r\n                 \"INDEPENDENT_PASS\", \"PUBLICATION_APPROVED\", \"RELEASED\", \"PRODUCTION_VERIFIED\"]\r\n@@ -177,8 +178,8 @@ def _seal(rec: dict[str, Any], prefix: str) -> dict[str, Any]:\r\n     \"\"\"\r\n     # `trace` is a derived relation, not body content: it carries the parent hash, so it cannot be\r\n     # inside the hash it references. The validator excludes exactly the same key set\r\n-    # (validate.NON_RECORD_SIDECARS) - one definition, referenced in both places.\r\n-    body = {k: v for k, v in rec.items() if k not in NON_RECORD_SIDECARS}\r\n+    # (validate.HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS) - one definition, referenced in both places.\r\n+    body = {k: v for k, v in rec.items() if k not in HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS}\r\n     digest = sha256_text(canonical_json(body))\r\n     rec[\"subject_id\"] = f\"{prefix}-{digest[:16]}\"\r\n     rec[\"content_hash\"] = digest\r\ndiff --git a/src/pipd_ls_sp/validate.py b/src/pipd_ls_sp/validate.py\r\nindex affd824..9eee94a 100644\r\n--- a/src/pipd_ls_sp/validate.py\r\n+++ b/src/pipd_ls_sp/validate.py\r\n@@ -15,13 +15,26 @@ CLAIM_LADDER = [\"PROMPT_COMPILE_PASS\", \"HGK_ADMITTED\", \"RUNTIME_READY\", \"LOCAL_Q\r\n                 \"INDEPENDENT_PASS\", \"PUBLICATION_APPROVED\", \"RELEASED\", \"PRODUCTION_VERIFIED\"]\r\n RELEASE_BOUND = (\"PI-PKG\", \"PD-PKG\", \"ECP\", \"TQAEP\", \"ConstructionContract\")\r\n \r\n-# The SINGLE definition of the non-record sidecar keys (CE-2 structural repair): fields the\r\n-# pipeline emits for the operator/registry but that are NOT part of a record's schema body.\r\n-# `_body_hash` (identity recomputation), `validate_record` (schema check on a stripped copy),\r\n-# `validate_bundle` and `pipeline` all reference THIS object, so the list cannot be silently\r\n-# split into a second copy again.\r\n-NON_RECORD_SIDECARS = (\"subject_id\", \"version\", \"content_hash\", \"schema_version\", \"trace\",\r\n-                       \"_profile_meta\")\r\n+# CE-2 refinement (checker counterexample CE-CODEX-TRACE-WIDENING). Two DISTINCT concepts that\r\n+# used to be conflated into one list: \"fields excluded from the body hash\" and \"fields that are\r\n+# not part of the record at all\". They overlap only on `_profile_meta`.\r\n+#\r\n+# HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS -- the fields the body hash does NOT cover: the universal identity fields\r\n+#   (recomputed after sealing), the derived `trace` relation (it carries the parent hash, so it\r\n+#   cannot sit inside the hash it references) and the `_profile_meta` sidecar. This is the\r\n+#   HASH-EXCLUSION set ONLY; it says nothing about which fields a schema declares. `_body_hash`\r\n+#   and `pipeline._seal` both reference THIS object, so the hash and its recomputation stay one\r\n+#   definition and cannot diverge.\r\n+HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS = (\"subject_id\", \"version\", \"content_hash\", \"schema_version\", \"trace\",\r\n+                        \"_profile_meta\")\r\n+\r\n+# NON_RECORD_SIDECARS -- the fields the pipeline emits for the operator/registry that NO schema\r\n+#   declares at all. Today that is exactly `_profile_meta`. `validate_record` strips ONLY this\r\n+#   set before the schema check, so a field that is hash-excluded but IS declared by the schema\r\n+#   (the identity fields, or `trace` on PI-PKG) remains schema-enforced, and an undeclared\r\n+#   `trace` on PD-PKG/ECP/TQAEP is again rejected by `additionalProperties: false`, exactly as\r\n+#   the published validator behaves.\r\n+NON_RECORD_SIDECARS = (\"_profile_meta\",)\r\n \r\n \r\n def _validator(schema: dict[str, Any]):\r\n@@ -35,17 +48,18 @@ def _validator(schema: dict[str, Any]):\r\n def validate_record(contract: str, record: dict[str, Any], schemas_dir: Path | None = None) -> list[str]:\r\n     \"\"\"Return a list of deterministic findings; empty list == schema-valid.\r\n \r\n-    Schema validation runs on a COPY of the record with the non-record sidecars removed, so a\r\n-    bundle assembled from the compilers' own stdout (which carries ``_profile_meta``) is held to\r\n-    its declared field set instead of tripping ``additionalProperties: false``. This is exactly\r\n-    the \"stripped before validation\" behavior ``pipeline`` already declares.\r\n-\r\n-    The sidecar list is the SAME ``NON_RECORD_SIDECARS`` object the hash path uses. A key on that\r\n-    list that the SCHEMA ITSELF declares (the identity fields and ``trace`` are declared *and\r\n-    required* properties) is NOT removed: it is part of the record's contract, so its presence\r\n-    must still be schema-enforced. Only sidecars the schema does not declare - i.e. the genuine\r\n-    non-record sidecar ``_profile_meta`` - are stripped. This closes CE-2 without weakening any\r\n-    required-field or ``additionalProperties`` check.\r\n+    Schema validation runs on a COPY of the record with the genuine non-record sidecars\r\n+    (``NON_RECORD_SIDECARS`` - today only ``_profile_meta``) removed, so a bundle assembled from\r\n+    the compilers' own stdout (which carries ``_profile_meta``) is held to its declared field set\r\n+    instead of tripping ``additionalProperties: false``. This is exactly the \"stripped before\r\n+    validation\" behavior ``pipeline`` already declares.\r\n+\r\n+    ``NON_RECORD_SIDECARS`` is deliberately NOT the same set as ``HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS``: a field\r\n+    can be excluded from the body hash (``trace``, the identity fields) yet still be part of the\r\n+    record's contract, so its presence must remain schema-enforced. Only the genuine non-record\r\n+    sidecar ``_profile_meta`` is stripped; the defensive \"unless the schema declares it\" guard\r\n+    keeps that safe even if a future schema chooses to declare a sidecar. This closes CE-2 without\r\n+    widening the accepted input.\r\n     \"\"\"\r\n     schema = registry.load_schema(contract, schemas_dir)\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
.hgk\rounds\R5R-20261010-s4-ce-repair-and-codex-verify\verify-codex-2\verdict.json:27:      "evidence": "Read validate.py, pipeline.py and registry.py directly from the new wheel; compared with published bytes and pre-repair tracked source, without executing the superseded wheel. NON_RECORD_SIDECARS is now exactly (_profile_meta,), while HASH_EXCLUDED_FIELDS retains the old six-field hash exclusion set and is shared by pipeline._seal and validator._body_hash. validate_record preserves every other field for schema validation and retains its declared-property guard. All 19 packaged schemas are byte-identical to published; all declare all four identity fields. For those schemas, shrinking the stripping set changes only the acceptance of undeclared trace, and rejects no schema-legitimate input previously accepted. PI-PKG trace remains declared and its original compiler trace passes. _profile_meta is genuinely operator/profile metadata added after record sealing, absent from all 19 record schemas, and excluded from the digest; tolerance is NOT a PI-PKG special case. Adding it to PD-PKG, ECP or TQAEP also passes with unchanged hashes. A workspace schema declaring _profile_meta as string correctly rejects the compiler object sidecar instead of stripping it. Observation disclosed: PI-PKG trace has schema {}, so an object-shaped trace also passes both new and published validators (published probe removes only the known sidecar to isolate behavior). This unchanged declared-property permissiveness is not the undeclared-trace widening."
.hgk\rounds\R5R-20261010-s4-ce-repair-and-codex-verify\verify-codex\raw_transcript.txt:3146:  "body": "# PIPD-LS-SP `v0.1.0-preview.1` — owner-authorised S4 open-source preview (BETA)\n\n**This is a prerelease, not a production release.** It is the S4 *pre-implementation / pre-dev*\ncontract compiler, published so that the licence grant, the packaging and the user path can be\nchecked against real bytes. Read the **Known limitations** below before using it for anything.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Licence | **Apache-2.0** — owner grant, round R5Q, 2026-10-10 (`OWNER_LICENSE_DECISION.yaml`) |\n| Release commit | `cc9bf574c3b3f0f44ea615b5c67ae40d74efee32` (tree ``) |\n| Wheel build-input commit | `c66be0806155eeae3c997a905885ede1e661571f` |\n| Review baseline (unchanged) | `r5p-post-challenge-repair` @ `2efc84eac8e1939092c748d5b389b6dd72267aef` |\n| Frozen candidate head | `7c5bc585c7d889cd338da853be4efc4b8f07d3b2` |\n| Default branch `main` | R3 content **preserved**, advanced only by a front-page pointer commit (`3aebbbce` → `de3a1d9`) |\n\nThe earlier review wheel `bb070a6f…` is **superseded**. Licence metadata\nchanged the member composition, so the published wheel is a different artefact; the identity\nmapping is `build_input_commit` → `released_commit` → wheel SHA256, never a reused hash.\n`candidate_head` is the frozen R5P review candidate and is deliberately **not** the release tip.\n\n## Install\n\nRequires Python >= 3.11. The wheel declares `jsonschema>=4.0`.\n\n```sh\npython -m venv venv\nvenv/bin/python -m pip install pipd_ls_sp-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl      # Windows: venv\\Scripts\\python\nvenv/bin/python -m pipd_ls_sp.cli doctor\n```\n\n`doctor` must report the schema source it actually loaded. The 19 JSON Schemas travel inside the\nwheel, so an installed `pipd` is self-sufficient — there is no `PYTHONPATH` step.\n\n**`validate` is the exception — pass `--root`.** Unlike `doctor`, which resolves the schemas that\nship inside the wheel, `validate` looks for `schemas/` under the current working directory unless you\ngive it the global `--root`. Installed and running from a directory that has no `schemas/`, this\nfails with `INPUT_SHAPE_INVALID` before it ever looks at your bundle:\n\n```sh\nvenv/bin/python -m pipd_ls_sp.cli --root <site-packages>/pipd_ls_sp validate --bundle bundle.json\n```\n\nThis is limitation 6 below. It is a documented prerequisite here because the behaviour is surprising,\nnot because it is the intended design.\n\n## Verify the bytes before you trust them\n\n```sh\nsha256sum -c SHA256SUMS\n```\n\nAttached assets:\n\n| Asset | SHA256 |\n|---|---|\n| `pipd_ls_sp-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl` | `c450dbef1c3bfbc2048dcf0562f83d655cc5e5940511e65fd44bf9f91cf14e60` |\n| `pipd-ls-sp-v0.1.0-preview.1-source.zip` | `8faaaa5a171c3ba62e5483e2418854936965015ef976dfb92a74cffa569659b0` |\n| `SHA256SUMS` | — |\n| `PREVIEW_NOTES_v0.1.0-preview.1.md` | — |\n\nInside the wheel, `pipd_ls_sp-0.1.0.dist-info/licenses/` carries `LICENSE`, `NOTICE` and\n`OWNER_LICENSE_DECISION.yaml` — the licence basis travels with the binary, so the SPDX id in the\nmetadata can be traced back to the grant that produced it instead of being taken on faith.\n\n## Licence and rights\n\n- **Granted (Apache-2.0):** the agent-authored engineering output — `src/`, `tests/`, `tools/`,\n  `schemas/`, `fixtures/`, `skills/`, `docs/`, `dist/` — and the derived expression authored by the\n  governed build from the owner's own governing PIPD corpus.\n- **Not asserted:** the three donor Skill plugins (`SDLC_PRW_HLPE` R2, `SWOF_ECP` v2.0.1,\n  `SWOF_TQAEP` v2.0.1) are inventoried as **SOURCE references only**; no verbatim copy is claimed.\n  See `PROVENANCE.md`.\n- **Third-party:** dependencies keep their own licences (`jsonschema` = MIT). The SBOM lists the\n  declared dependency set; it does **not** compute a transitive closure, and says so in its own\n  scope property.\n- **Exclusion policy:** any file later shown to carry third-party rights leaves the grant from the\n  moment it is re-proven. Published tags are never rewritten — a superseding release is issued.\n\n## Known limitations\n\nRead these as part of the licence grant, not as fine print.\n\n1. **`DEL-018 RELEASE_MANIFEST@1` remains `FAIL / EVIDENCE_GAP`.** The check\n   `tools/build_publication_manifest.py --check --current` exits 1 and is **not** closed by this\n   release. Three test files are not covered by it: `test_git_object_reader.py`,\n   `test_doctor_schema_truth.py`, `test_tqaep_design_positive.py`. It is disclosed here rather than\n   silently upgraded to a release-manifest PASS.\n2. **`PARTIAL_CHALLENGE` is maintained.** The 57-row base remains\n   `28 evidenced / 10 active gaps / 19 deferred`. Only the rows that affect this preview's\n   publication and user path were validated.\n3. **TQAEP is a design contract only.** This plugin emits pre-implementation contracts. It does not\n   execute HGK WorkOrders and does not run a formal checker acceptance on your behalf. Do not read\n   the artefacts as evidence that anything was built, deployed or verified downstream.\n4. **Windows, host-native and S5–S8 are not certified.** Junction/symlink behaviour, host-native\n   projections and live runtime stages were not exercised. Nothing here certifies them.\n5. **The scope is construction-time only.** S4 produces contracts for work that has not been done.\n6. **`validate` does not resolve the wheel's own schemas by default.** `doctor` finds the schemas\n   installed inside the wheel (`schema_source.mode = INSTALLED`); `validate` instead looks for\n   `schemas/` under the current working directory. Run from a foreign directory with no `--root`,\n   `validate` exits 2 with `INPUT_SHAPE_INVALID` / `FileNotFoundError: <cwd>/schemas/PI-PKG.schema.json`\n   even though `doctor` in the same environment is perfectly happy. Pass the global\n   `--root <site-packages>/pipd_ls_sp` as in the Install section above. Found by the independent\n   verifier, reproduced by the maker against the published wheel bytes.\n7. **A bundle assembled from the compilers' own stdout is rejected by `validate`.** `compile-pi`\n   emits a top-level `_profile_meta` sidecar, but the `PI-PKG` schema sets\n   `additionalProperties: false`, so feeding the four unmodified compiler outputs straight into\n   `validate` exits 1 with\n   `PI-PKG/: Additional properties are not allowed ('_profile_meta' was unexpected)`.\n   Deleting only that sidecar makes the same bundle pass (`checked=4`, `findings=[]`). The tool\n   therefore rejects its own unedited output. Found by the independent verifier, reproduced by the\n   maker against the published wheel bytes.\n\nLimitations 6 and 7 are the two counter-examples the independent verifier returned. Both are\n**non-stop-ship** — install, licence packaging, schema resolution by `doctor`, the CLI and the\nartefact hashes are all unaffected — but they sit on the boundary of the owner grant's stop-ship\ncondition *\"core intake → PI → PD → ECP/TQAEP chain broadly unusable\"*, so they are stated plainly\nrather than filed as cosmetic. They are **open**, not fixed: fixing either changes member bytes and\ntherefore requires a superseding release, which published tags are never rewritten to carry.\n\n## What this release does not claim\n\n`FULL_S0_S4_INDEPENDENT_PASS`, `ALL_57_SPECS_VERIFIED`, `ALL_HOSTS_LIVE_CERTIFIED`,\n`S5_RUNTIME_READY`, `G_RELEASE_FULL_PASS` and `PRODUCTION_VERIFIED` are **NOT GRANTED**.\n\nThe highest claim this round can reach is `OWNER_AUTHORIZED_OPEN_SOURCE_PREVIEW_BETA_PUBLISHED` —\na published, licenced, byte-verifiable preview.\n\n**Independent acceptance: received, with qualifications.** A verifier that did not participate in the\nbuild re-derived the published artefact from scratch — its own anonymous download and its own git\nclone, no local cache — and returned **9/9 claims PASS with no stop-ship counter-example**. Two\nqualifications attach and are not glossed over: (a) it ran on a **different model lane than the one\nthis round's plan named**, so no planned-model separation-of-duties is claimed; and (b) the receipt\ncovers the **immutable** artefact — tag, commit, tree, wheel bytes — while the mutable surfaces on\nthis page and the repository front page are covered by a timestamped readback instead. The receipt\nitself did not find the two limitations 6 and 7 above: a **second, later** independent run found\nthose, and the maker reproduced both directly against the published wheel bytes.\n\nNothing above upgrades this to `FULL_S0_S4_INDEPENDENT_PASS`. Treat every maker-side PASS as\ncorroborated **only** for the immutable released artefact.\n\n## Reporting\n\nOpen an issue on this repository with the version (`v0.1.0-preview.1`), the command, the exit code and the\nexact output. Real counter-examples start an affected-only repair round; this preview's scope is\ndeliberately narrow, so please include the reproduction.\n"
